id: interface/perspective-field-and-feedback-reintegration-v0.1
title: "Perspective field, answer frontier, and feedback reintegration"
kind: interface-learning-boundary-contract
status: proposed-v0.1
version: 0.1.0
recorded: 2026-09-05
mutationAuthorized: false
externalEffectsAuthorized: false

purpose: >
  Render one question, claim, expression, or candidate move together with the
  attributed standpoints that bear on it; preserve competing, complementary,
  conditional, and unresolved answers; explain how those answers inform the
  next warranted turn; and reintegrate human, Agent, audience, and world
  feedback without converting popularity, agreement, emotion, or model output
  into truth, reward, authority, or proof.

product_compression:
  statement: >
    Ask the question, see what each relevant perspective says, inspect what
    supports or challenges each answer, and understand why the current model
    recommends a question, hold, test, or move next.
  creator_case: >
    A creator can publish one provisioned expression, receive attributed public
    responses through the respondents' own apertures, and use those responses
    to refine the model, audience, expression, experiment, or future move while
    preserving what was actually said and what remains unproven.

core_definitions:
  question: >
    A typed projection of a lacuna or decision-relevant uncertainty. A question
    may remain open, be decomposed, depend on another question, or reopen when
    context, participants, evidence, purpose, or authority changes.
  perspective: >
    A bounded standpoint defined by an attributed participant or simulated
    source, role, relationship, access aperture, context, time, and relevant
    incentives or limitations. A perspective is not automatically an Agent,
    identity, personality, or truth source.
  answer_candidate: >
    An attributed proposition, conditional response, observation, preference,
    request, refusal, hypothesis, or unknown offered in relation to one or more
    questions. It remains a candidate until its semantic status is explicitly
    updated through ratification, evidence, consequence, or proof.
  voice: >
    An expression event: what an attributed standpoint says here, through this
    aperture, about this question, claim, relationship, expression, or move. A
    voice is not automatically a person, psychological part, Agent, Yawnbot, or
    stable belief.
  comment: >
    An external or internal expression event attached to a published or private
    projection. The original text, author or anonymous session, target
    projection revision, timestamp, channel, and provisioning remain
    recoverable where policy permits.
  reaction: >
    A low-bandwidth signal such as agree, disagree, slider position, like,
    dislike, dwell, share, or click. It may indicate recognition, attention,
    preference, affect, or willingness; it does not establish truth, quality,
    causation, or principal values by itself.
  feedback_event: >
    An attributed event bearing on a question, answer, expression, move,
    prediction, experience, or outcome. Feedback may be evidence, but only after
    its relation, source access, scope, independence, and quality are modeled.
  answer_frontier: >
    A time-indexed ranked View of the answer candidates currently most relevant
    to resolving a question or selecting the next warranted turn. It is not a
    permanent containment hierarchy or a vote tally.
  synthesis: >
    An attributed integrator projection that states the current leading model,
    meaningful alternatives, unresolved disagreements, and missing evidence.
    It may compress plurality but may not erase it.

question_answer_move_spine:
  sequence:
    - active_question_or_lacuna
    - perspective_discovery_and_permission_filtering
    - attributed_answer_candidates
    - answer_relation_and_evidence_graph
    - ranked_answer_frontier
    - current_synthesis_with_disagreement
    - next_warranted_turn_selection
    - principal_dialogue_and_choice
    - authorized_move_hold_or_publication
    - consequence_and_external_response
    - feedback_classification_as_proposal
    - proof_and_calibration
    - updated_questions_answers_moves_and_apertures
  boundary: >
    This is a phase and View inside the existing Crystallization, Learning, and
    Execution loops. It is not a fourth canonical loop.

perspective_origins:
  may_include:
    - principal_current_self_report
    - principal_historical_self
    - affected_human_participant
    - invited_collaborator
    - local_yawnbot_attention_claim
    - connected_specialist_agent
    - advocate_or_challenger_role
    - external_commenter_or_audience_segment
    - research_or_measurement_source
    - model_simulated_perspective
    - explicit_unknown_or_missing_perspective
  identity_rule: >
    A human, Agent, Yawnbot, role, and simulated perspective remain distinct.
    A model may simulate what an absent perspective might say only when labeled
    simulated, source-bounded, non-consensual-as-representation, and without
    impersonation or authority.
  yawnbot_rule: >
    An existing Yawnbot may produce an attributed answer from its current Yawn
    and accepted purpose. A generated voice does not become a Yawnbot unless it
    independently earns persistent identity, memory, responsibility,
    relationships, capability boundaries, authority, and an orientation/proof
    loop.

answer_candidate_contract:
  required_fields:
    - answer_candidate_id
    - question_refs
    - exact_content_or_source_ref
    - asserted_by_or_simulated_by
    - perspective_ref
    - source_refs
    - aperture_or_view_ref
    - role_and_relationship_context
    - epistemic_status
    - applicability_scope_or_conditions
    - confidence_or_unknown
    - conviction_or_preference_if_expressed
    - visibility_and_provisioning
    - created_at
    - effective_time
    - lifecycle_status
  recommended_fields:
    - incentive_or_conflict_disclosure
    - direct_experience_scope
    - supporting_evidence_refs
    - weakening_or_contradictory_evidence_refs
    - independence_or_correlation_group
    - affectedness_or_standing
    - freshness
    - what_would_change_it
    - supersedes_or_is_superseded_by
    - proposed_question_rank_impact
    - proposed_move_rank_impact
  separation_rule: >
    Confidence, conviction, popularity, salience, affectedness, expertise,
    evidence quality, and decision relevance answer different questions and may
    not be collapsed into one score.

answer_relation_graph:
  relation_types:
    - supports
    - weakens
    - contradicts
    - reframes
    - qualifies
    - elaborates
    - applies_under
    - depends_on
    - subsumes
    - compatible_with
    - addresses_different_scope
    - asks_for_evidence
    - unresolved_against
  hierarchy_rule: >
    Use parent/child answer structure only when one answer explicitly decomposes,
    scopes, or subsumes another. Otherwise preserve a relational field. The UI
    may rank answers for current relevance without pretending that rank is
    ontological containment.

answer_frontier_ranking:
  hard_gates:
    - safety
    - rightful_authority_and_consent
    - privacy_visibility_and_egress
    - source_and_identity_integrity
    - proof_integrity
    - applicability_to_active_question
  factors:
    - evidential_support
    - source_access_and_directness
    - independence_and_correlation
    - expertise_or_capability_fit
    - affectedness_and_legitimate_standing
    - scope_fit
    - recency_and_freshness
    - incentive_or_conflict_risk
    - contradiction_strength
    - expected_information_gain
    - decision_relevance
    - cost_of_further_inquiry
  excluded_as_sole_basis:
    - popularity
    - number_of_agents
    - emotional_intensity
    - user_agreement
    - model_eloquence
    - engagement
  output: >
    Show the leading answer or synthesis only when warranted, meaningful
    alternatives, material dissent, unresolved unknowns, and why the ordering
    currently holds. A minority answer with strong direct evidence may outrank
    a popular unsupported answer.

question_hierarchy_and_move_derivation:
  question_frontier: >
    Rank questions by hard gates, expected orientation gain, information value,
    relationship consequence, decision criticality, answer burden, and proof
    relevance.
  answer_frontier: >
    For a selected question, rank or cluster answer candidates while preserving
    conditionality, plurality, and disagreement.
  move_frontier: >
    Derive candidate moves from the current synthesis, accepted values and
    constraints, authority, time, resources, and unresolved risk. Every material
    move cites the questions, answer candidates, evidence, and assumptions that
    support it.
  uncertainty_rule: >
    When the answer frontier is too uncertain, the highest-value move may be to
    ask, measure, inspect, consult, publish a probe, or hold rather than choose a
    substantive action.

public_feedback_reintegration:
  publication_binding:
    requires:
      - publication_or_expression_id
      - yawn_and_revision_ref
      - aperture_and_presentation_ref
      - intended_audience_or_unknown
      - intended_outcome_or_question
      - provisioning_and_source_boundary
      - published_at
  preserve_raw_event:
    fields:
      - exact_feedback_or_content_addressed_source
      - author_or_anonymous_session_ref
      - channel_and_context
      - target_projection_revision
      - timestamp
      - reaction_metrics_as_separate_signals
      - collection_method_and_terms_or_permission_status
  proposed_feedback_kinds:
    - experience_report
    - factual_claim_or_correction
    - question_or_confusion
    - preference_or_request
    - value_or_norm_disagreement
    - support_or_resonance
    - objection_or_counterexample
    - outcome_or_consequence_report
    - manipulation_marketing_or_incentivized_input
    - abuse_spam_or_irrelevant_input
    - unknown
  weighting_dimensions:
    - relevance_to_published_question_or_claim
    - direct_experience
    - evidence_quality
    - affectedness
    - expertise_fit
    - independence
    - representativeness_or_sampling_limit
    - incentive_and_conflict
    - novelty
    - adversarial_strength
    - fit_to_intended_audience
  update_targets_must_remain_distinct:
    - canonical_model
    - communication_or_presentation
    - audience_model
    - objective_or_value_proposal
    - question_frontier
    - move_or_experiment_frontier
    - confidence_and_calibration
    - no_change
  rule: >
    Public feedback first creates attributed proposals. It cannot silently
    update principal values, objectives, canonical truth, Yawnbot policy,
    reward functions, or effect authority.

learning_and_reward_boundary:
  preferred_description: >
    Call the mechanism attributed feedback reintegration or
    perspective-conditioned model revision unless a formal reward, policy, and
    update rule actually exists.
  separate_signals:
    recognition: "the user says the voice resembles something they experience"
    conviction: "the user currently endorses a proposition"
    preference: "the user likes or chooses an option"
    clarity: "the audience reports understanding"
    resonance: "the audience experiences relevance or emotional fit"
    execution: "the move occurred"
    consequence: "something happened afterward"
    proof: "evidence bears on the prediction or success condition"
    value: "a rightful principal or affected participant evaluates an outcome"
  reward_rule: >
    Do not derive a reward function from likes, comments, agreement, retention,
    or praise. Any policy update must name the objective, rightful principal,
    evidence, affected parties, safety/authority gates, scope, and rollback or
    falsifier.
  creator_guard: >
    The goal is not to eliminate negative comments or maximize positive ones.
    Optimize the explicitly chosen outcome—such as clarity for an intended
    audience, factual accuracy, useful action, or value alignment—while
    preserving principled disagreement and minority evidence.

adversarial_deliberation:
  relation_to_advocate_challenger: >
    Advocate and challenger are useful roles inside the perspective field. They
    must be attributed and may be correlated when generated by one model.
    External perspectives may supply genuine independent challenge, but social
    disagreement is not automatically high-quality adversarial evidence.
  no_forced_pair: >
    Do not force every question into a binary position/counter-position. Some
    answer fields contain several alternatives, complementary answers,
    conditional branches, or a leading unknown. Do not manufacture symmetry.

aperture_and_provisioning:
  rule: >
    The perspective field is a registered View. Each viewer sees only the
    answer candidates, sources, comments, identities, and evidence permitted by
    effective grants. A public comment may be visible while the private model it
    influenced remains hidden.
  perspective_aperture: >
    Show what each participant could access when speaking. A comment based on a
    public story should not be presented as if the commenter had access to the
    private Yawn.
  personal_presentation: >
    One user may prefer voices, another a table, graph, debate, question tree,
    or game-world scene. The underlying attributed records and operator
    semantics remain identical.

history_and_replay:
  preserve:
    - question_and_candidate_set_at_time
    - answer_candidates_and_sources
    - ranking_or_clustering_policy
    - perspective_access_aperture
    - principal_response
    - publication_revision
    - incoming_feedback
    - proposed_and_accepted_updates
    - consequence_and_proof
  invariant: >
    Replay reconstructs the recorded field and why the synthesis or move changed.
    It may not regenerate historical voices with a newer model and present them
    as the original deliberation.

current_codebase_pressure_test:
  useful_seeds:
    - >
      lib/agents/feed/surface-voice-generator.ts already generates position and
      counter-position pairs, needs, tensions, and constraints.
    - >
      lib/agents/feed/feedback-reintegration-graph.ts already attempts to map
      public feedback into lacunae and future artifacts.
    - >
      lib/agents/answer-agent processes public answers and connects them to
      questions and axes.
    - >
      lib/agents/expression implements Expression(t)=f(Tree(t)), preserving the
      important intuition that answers sharpen an expression.
  required_corrections:
    - >
      Voice is currently over-specialized as internal psychology and often
      assigned bias, need, emotion, threat, or IFS-part labels without user
      ratification. These remain attributed hypotheses, never diagnoses.
    - >
      Position/counter pairs are forced even when the answer space is not binary
      or evidentially symmetric.
    - >
      VoiceSource is only system or user and cannot represent named humans,
      Yawnbots, Agents, historical selves, public commenters, providers,
      research sources, or simulation status with adequate provenance.
    - >
      Agree/disagree and slider responses are described as accumulating
      confidence in the self-model. Recognition, conviction, preference, model
      confidence, and truth must be separated.
    - >
      SurfacePhaseContainer gates continuation on acknowledging a voice. A user
      must be able to reject all candidates, remain unsure, hold, or continue
      without false ratification.
    - >
      The control-level detector uses keyword heuristics to place input into a
      fixed Regulation/Task/Goal/Identity/Meaning hierarchy. Treat this as a
      transparent routing proposal, not ontology, diagnosis, or permanent user
      type; preserve mixed levels and user override.
    - >
      Feedback reintegration uses brittle keyword rules such as "but" for
      objection and "?" for confusion, and may create artifacts immediately.
      Classification and artifact creation must remain proposals until governed.
    - >
      Positive feedback is described as validating a direction. It supports a
      bounded claim about resonance with that respondent, not truth or outcome.
    - >
      The Answer Agent applies positive coherence deltas and even a positive
      fallback when extraction fails. Feedback may increase, decrease, split,
      dispute, or leave coherence unchanged; processing failure must not produce
      epistemic credit.
    - >
      Expression synthesis produces one sharp coherent statement and increases
      confidence with answer count and coherence. The expression is a View; it
      must preserve dissent, unknowns, source scope, and disconfirming evidence,
      and density may not substitute for accuracy.
  migration_rule: >
    Preserve existing rows and interfaces through compatibility adapters. Add
    richer provenance and status before changing labels or confidence semantics.
    Do not destructively reinterpret old reactions or generated voices as
    ratified beliefs.

acceptance_tests:
  - "A voice can be shown without asserting that it is an Agent, Yawnbot, psychological part, or current belief."
  - "An existing Yawnbot can submit an attributed answer candidate from its current Yawn and purpose."
  - "A model-simulated absent human perspective is visibly simulated and cannot impersonate or authorize for that person."
  - "A question can display three complementary answers and one unknown without forcing binary opposition."
  - "Question priority, answer support, social popularity, user conviction, salience, and move leverage remain distinct."
  - "A user's agree/slider response updates only the declared recognition, conviction, or preference record."
  - "Rejecting every generated voice remains a valid outcome and creates a lacuna rather than blocking continuation."
  - "A processing failure creates no positive coherence, confidence, or reward update."
  - "Supportive but false feedback does not outrank a well-evidenced critical correction."
  - "A high-quality minority counterexample remains visible despite low popularity."
  - "Public feedback is bound to the exact publication revision and the commenter's available aperture."
  - "A public comment creates a proposal, not an automatic canonical model or move mutation."
  - "A move can cite the questions, answer candidates, evidence, values, constraints, and authority that derived it."
  - "Replay explains why an answer or move changed without regenerating the historical field."
  - "Optimizing communication clarity does not silently change the principal's values or underlying factual claim."
  - "A public perspective field cannot reveal private evidence or enumerate hidden participants."

comparative_tests:
  conditions:
    - single_model_answer
    - forced_binary_voice_pair
    - multi_answer_perspective_field
    - independent_advocate_challenger
    - public_feedback_reintegration
  measures:
    - correction_burden
    - question_information_gain
    - decision_understanding
    - minority_evidence_retention
    - calibration
    - repeated_error_rate
    - source_and_aperture_comprehension
    - user_agreement_bias
    - false_balance_rate
    - automatic_mutation_rate
    - cognitive_carrying_reduction

falsifiers:
  - "The perspective field adds performative voices without improving questions, moves, or calibration."
  - "Users mistake generated voices for their own beliefs or other people's actual minds."
  - "Social popularity dominates evidence or rightful values."
  - "The system learns to seek praise, agreement, or engagement rather than the declared outcome."
  - "Answer ranking hides consequential minority evidence."
  - "Maintaining source, aperture, and incentive metadata costs more cognition than the field returns."
  - "Public feedback leaks private context or creates unauthorized policy changes."

links:
  - core/orientation.yawn
  - core/inquiry-selection.yawn
  - core/projection-and-aperture.yawn
  - interface/ranked-move-deliberation-v0.1.yawn
  - interface/aperture-registry-and-provisioning-v0.1.yawn
  - interface/yawnbot-agentic-embodiment-v0.1.yawn
  - records/2026-09-05-voices-answers-and-public-feedback-clarification.yawn
